Sparky Stereo OS: libf3d as shared libraries (libf3d3 and libf3d-dev, which Debian does not ship), so that the viewer and the
thumbnailers can use it. libvtkext, private to libf3d, goes in /usr/lib/<multiarch>/f3d.

render.stereo draws the two eyes of a stereo window, declared by the edition's Mesa as one full side-by-side surface under
Stereo KWin, and of an image (renderToImage returns the left eye and the right eye side by side, left first). A UI only
frame of a stereo window draws the scene again, which fixed one eye shown in both buffers. The program's 3D options are
the contract's: --stereo is "Enable 3D" (on by default through /etc/f3d/config.d/50_sparky_stereo.json, F3
switches it), F4 opens the Display Settings, --stereo-eye-angle is gone, and our sentences are in the ten languages of the
edition.

The separation of the two eyes is the desktop's: it follows the person's depth limits that Stereo KWin gives the window
(libstereo-declare 1.11, _KDE_NET_WM_STEREO_DEPTH_LIMITS), the largest one under which the nearest and the farthest point of the
scene stay inside the sunk and popped limits, and follows a change of them while the window is open; the fixed 2 degrees of
+stereo3d1 is gone from the window. With no limits (an older compositor, limits not delivered yet) the two eyes are the same
picture and the program says so in the ten languages. The pair of a thumbnail keeps its 2 degrees (its depth has no recorded
design yet).

f3d-thumbnail-pair draws a model as a stereo pair (two parallel, sheared cameras) marked with X-KDE::Stereo3DLayout, and
the thumbnailer files call it and name the canonical MIME types (KIO matches a thumbnailer to the type's own name).

The separation of a 3D window is the person's depth limits and nothing else: render.stereo_eye_angle is not used, from -D, a
configuration file or libf3d's API, and whoever sets it is told once in a warning in the ten languages of the edition (+stereo3d2
still took the separation from that option when it was set, so -D render.stereo_eye_angle=6 gave 104 and 63 pixels against limits
that allow 2 and 6). The pair of an image keeps its 2 degrees by window::setStereoImageEyeAngle, which is not an option.

A program that shows the scene inside its own window can make the GLX window a child of its window (window::setParentWindowId) and read the
window's id (window::getNativeWindowId): the 3D area of a Qt viewer is positioned and sized by the program, and the program can declare what it
needs on that window. Nothing changes for a program that does not call them (patch 0010).
